<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ARSA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arakan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohingya">Rohingya</a> Salvation Army is a Rohingya insurgent group in Rakhine State, Myanmar. Its attacks on police posts in October 2016 and August 2017 were followed by military 'clearance operations' in which thousands of Rohingya were killed and more than 700,000 fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. The Myanmar government designated it a terrorist organisation in 2017.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to a spokesman, the group was founded in 2013 as Harakah al-Yaqin, the Faith Movement, after the 2012 Rakhine State riots. A December 2016 report by the International Crisis Group said it was led by Ataullah abu Ammar Jununi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohingya">Rohingya</a> born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and raised in Saudi Arabia, with a committee of émigrés in Saudi Arabia. Before 2016 its members patrolled villages with bamboo sticks; some months before its first attack it began recruiting men to train across the border in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. It took its present name in a statement of 29 March 2017.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its attacks of October 2016 on posts along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>–Myanmar border left nine border officers and four soldiers dead. On 25 August 2017 it struck at least two dozen police posts; the government counted 77 insurgents and 12 members of the security forces killed. Myanmar's army blamed it for the killing of 99 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> at Kha Maung Seik that day, and Amnesty International reported in May 2018 that it had evidence of the massacre. ARSA denied such charges. It declared a one-month ceasefire on 9 September 2017, which the government rejected.</p><p>In the Bangladeshi refugee camps the group was accused of killing critics and community leaders, among them Mohammed Mohib Ullah, killed on 29 September 2021; Bangladesh Police named ARSA as responsible, which it denied. From 2024 it cooperated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatmadaw">Tatmadaw</a> against the Arakan Army, and was accused of forcing young men and boys to fight. Bangladesh's Rapid Action Battalion arrested Ataullah in Narayanganj on 18 March 2025.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>ARSA calls itself an ethno-nationalist and secular group with no links to foreign Islamists, though it seeks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> from foreign Muslim clerics. Unlike other insurgent groups in Myanmar it has no ranks or uniforms, and in August 2017 most of its fighters carried machetes and bamboo sticks. Analysts have compared its tactics to those of insurgents in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>: small raids across a border, then retreat to a community of the same background. Most of its written statements are in English.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
